  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $N$ containing all functions such that $f(0)=0.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $N$ is closed under function addition\n- $N$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $N$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $N$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, suppose $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are functions from $N.$ Then $f(x)=g(x)=0$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(0) & =f(0)+g(0) \\\\  & =0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $N$ is closed under function addition.\n\n- Statement II is true.  Indeed, suppose $f(x)\\in N$  and let $\\alpha \\in R.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha f)(0) & =\\alpha \\cdot f(0) \\\\  & =\\alpha \\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $N$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $N$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"",
